  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nIntermittent showers and storms will persist Friday into Saturday as \nlow level QG convergence and theta-e advection slowly move eastward. \nWhile it will not be days full of rainfall, the days will be more \ncommon to the ones we have had recently with plenty of clouds and \ntimes of precipitation. While there will be instability for\noccasional storms, the higher instability and overlapping shear\nis displaced west of the area. Thus, locally heavy rainfall \ncontinues to be the main concern due to the antecedent wet \nconditions. While soils will have a 24 to 48 hours break with \ndry conditions, infiltration capacity will remain reduced along\nwith elevated streamflows. Rainfall totals still look on track \nbetween 1 and 3 inches on the high end with ensemble \nprobabilities of a 1\" of rainfall in 24 hours between 45 and 55%\nover western Iowa Friday morning into Saturday morning \ndwindling to 20 to 30% chance over central Iowa Saturday morning\nthrough Sunday morning. A break in the rainfall will arrive as \nanother shortwave trough lifting northeastward or eastward moves\nthrough the region. This should bring large scale subsidence \ninto the area on Sunday providing drier conditions that may last\ninto at least Monday. Temperatures late this week into early \nthis weekend will remain on the cool side.",
